    <notes>Hurrian Hymn to Nikkal, h.6.
Tablet RS 15.30+15.49+17.387, National Museum, Damascus, from the royal
palace of Ugarit, c. 1400 BC: the oldest substantially complete notated piece
of music. The notation is a list of Akkadian interval names, each naming two
strings of a nine-string lyre, each followed by a number. Text after Laroche
(Ugaritica V, 1968) and Dietrich &amp; Loretz (Ugarit-Forschungen 7, 1975);
string names and the nīd qablim tuning after M. L. West, "The Babylonian
Musical Notation and the Hurrian Melodic Texts", Music &amp; Letters 75 (1994).
The colophon names the tuning nīd qablim.

Our realisation, from the tablet's data and no published version: the
second-named string of each pair is the melody and the number is how many
times it is struck (West's reading); the first-named string is the other hand,
struck with the pair's first note and again on each bar line while the pair
lasts; one strike per quarter at 72; strings 1–3 played an octave down, as
West suggests; one cymbal stroke per tablet line; the tuning cycle as a
prelude; a frame drum (tympanon, the kit's congas) on every bar, with a firmer
stroke where a tablet line begins; a double pipe (oboe) from line 6, holding
each melody note for as long as the lyre repeats it and breathing at the end
of each line. Line 5 is left to the lyre and drum, so the tablet's data is
heard plainly before the pipe joins. The tablet gives no rhythm and no fitting
of the 137 syllables of Hurrian text to the 72 notes, so there is no text
underlay.

The Hurrian text is given after Laroche (Ugaritica V, 1968) and Dietrich &amp;
Loretz (1975); the translation follows Thiel (1977) and Krispijn (2001) and is
tentative throughout — the language is a poorly understood local dialect and
the tablet has lacunae. The first line, in Laroche's syllabic transliteration
as revised by Dietrich &amp; Loretz:

ḫu-ma-ru-ḫat ú-wa-ri wa-an-da-ni-ta ú-ku-ri ku-ur-ku-ur-ta i-ša-al-la ú-la-li
ka-ap-ki ú-li-ib-ki še-re-et ú-nu-zu

In the usual pronunciation approximation: humarohat uweri wandanita kre
kurkurta izalla ulali kapki ulivki sret unozo.

The sense, after Thiel as revised by Krispijn: the speaker places an offering
of lead at the right foot of the divine throne, purifies and does away with
sin, and is at peace having made the sacrifice; the second section asks that
the offering and a libation of sesame oil win the goddess's heart; the third
names Nikkal as the one who gives fertility to the married woman, ending with
the voice of a woman who has borne no child. The hinge of the hymn, in Thiel's
words: "she will love me in her heart".</notes>
